diagnosis_text:
The user's report that host_1_1_1_1 lost all data-centre connectivity and that the switch_dist_1_1 uplink was the cause is not supported by any current or historical evidence in the lab.

A full four-phase diagnostic pass was completed:

**L2 / infrastructure (Phase A):** infra_sweep scanned all 15 devices and found zero ACL rules, zero static/poisoned ARP entries, no gateway or resolver divergence, and confirmed both hosts hold their correct IPs with valid default routes. l2_snapshot confirmed no duplicate MACs and no down or missing interfaces across the entire topology.

**Control plane / routing (Phase B):** ospf_snapshot confirmed all six FRR instances are running and healthy (vtysh reachable, both sockets present). Every router carries exactly 10 OSPF routes and every adjacency is in Full state — including the uplink the user suspected: switch_dist_1_1 eth0 (172.16.0.7/31) to router_core_1 eth2 (172.16.0.6/31) shows DR state, 1 neighbour, 1 full adjacency. tc_snapshot found no traffic-control shaping or netem corruption on any interface.

**Services (Phase D):** service_snapshot confirmed DNS resolves all four web hostnames correctly from host_1_1_1_1, and all four HTTP targets return 200 OK in under 5 ms. pressure_sweep found no stress-tool processes, no CPU pressure, and no socket saturation anywhere.

There is no flap script, no carrier-transition history, no OSPF neighbour-down log, and no mechanism that could produce the reported symptom. The network is fully healthy at this time; the reported outage is unconfirmed.

**Root cause:** No fault is present; the reported connectivity loss has left no evidence and no active fault mechanism exists.

**Fix:** No corrective action is needed. If the symptom recurs, capture the precise time and re-run this diagnostic while it is active.
